        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <B>OBJECTIVES:</B><BR>
The purpose of this study was to investigate the relationship between daily physical activity and bone density in young and elderly women. It is known that the rate of bone loss in the calcaneus (heel bone) is 0.2-0.3% per year beginning at age 35-40 years.  The hypothesis of his study is that declining physical activity is a contributor to age-related bone loss. We have developed a device (in-sole sensor) that quantifies the activity level in an objective manner so that we can determine the loading history of a bone through the daily exposure to ground reaction forces. This data is then applied to mathematical models for bone adaptation in response to loading histories.
<P>
<B>APPROACH:</B>
Forty-two female subjects, ranging in age from 25 to 74 years were placed into two self-selected activity groups (18 runners and 24 non-loading type of activities, i.e. swimming, cycling or no sport). Our ground reaction force monitor was fitted to the right foot of each subject and worn for five consecutive days.  The monitor measured average daily loading histories (GRFz). The density of the right heel was measured as close to the monitoring of activity as possible. A bone density index (BDI) was estimated from the average daily loading history and correlated with the heel density in each subject. The BDI was determine from [   ni  (     GRFzi  ) m ]1/(2m)  where activity is determined from i  loading conditions with n cycles of load per day,   is a subject specific scale factor to account for differences in body weight (BW) and GRF is the ith ground reaction force cycle peak load magnitude normalized to BW.
<P>
<B>RESULTS:</B>
<BR>
The observed number of gait related steps (>0.8 or 0.95 BW) was similar to data obtained by others with stepmeters or pedometers.Histograms of cycles of activity (load vs. frequency) could be made in terms of the subjects actual BW (‘relative’ load) or in terms of a BW scaled factor (absolute load). ‘Relative’ loading history is used when the intensity of the activity is compared among groups or individuals whereas ‘absolute’ loading histories are used to describe the actual force history. The histograms of higher body weight subjects showed a shift to higher GRFz loads when expressed in terms of  , the scaled factor, a more accurate description of actual applied forces. One goal of this study was to determine a value for m, a stress exponent relating GRFz history to Calcaneal Bone Mineral Density. The Bone Density Index was significantly correlated with the Calcaneal bone density (r = 0.437, p = 0.004).  Through least squares fitting, for all subjects, a stress component of 6.07 was determined as a value for m.Female volunteers were divided into high loaders (HL) and low loaders (LL) based on self-reported activity.  There were 18 HL with an average age of 42 yrs, height of 1.66 m, BW of 61.5 kg and BMI of 22.3.  There were 24 LL with an average age of 48, height of 1.63, BW of 73 and BMI of 27.3.  Both BW and BMI were greater in the LL than in the HL in both the young and elderly age groups.  The number of cycles of activity for more than 0.8 BW or 0.95 BW were higher in both elderly and young HL. However, for most activity parameters, elderly and young did not differ.  Activity did not appear to decrease with age.In contrast, calcaneal bone density (CBMD) was lower in the elderly than young (383 vs 446 mg/cm2).  In 20 elderly women, BW was a determinant for CBMD.  BW was not a determinant for CBMD in LL, but was in HL and only In 18 HL subjects, could activity be related to the CBMD through the Bone Density Index (r  = 0.59, p<0.009).  Conclusions: This study was inconclusive in terms of finding a relationship between activity grouping and bone density.  Nevertheless, we did find a significance to activity grouping when the BW is accounted for in the bone density measurement.  A Bone Density Index, determined from daily ground reaction force loading history is correlated with the calcaneal density or the CBMD/BW Scale Factor (m=6).<END>
